  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\nThe second half of the upcoming week looks much warmer and drier \nthanks to a shift in the upper-level flow pattern. A broad upper-\nlevel low will move into the western US on Wednesday.  Shortwave \nridging ahead of this feature should result in sunny skies with \nwarmer temperatures and allow for conditions to dry out after the \nrecent rainfall.  By Thursday, the upper-level trough will move into \nthe northern Plains. This would place the southern and central \nPlains in southwest flow aloft. Models are consistent in showing \nseveral waves rotating around the base of this trough Thursday \nthrough Sunday. This would push a series of systems through the \nsouthern Plains Thursday through Sunday which would interact with \nwarm, moist southerly flow at the surface. Models show the dryline \ngoing through it's typical diurnal cycle each day, which could \ngenerate chances for isolated thunderstorms each afternoon and \nevening.  In addition, the GFS shows a cold front dropping into \nnorth Texas on Sunday morning, but the ECMWF model does not. \nOverall, models are differing on where to place key surface \nfeatures, such as the surface front and the dryline, and each would \nbe a focus for thunderstorm development each day. Given the low \nconfidence due to model differences, precipitation chances were kept \nlow until better agreement occurs.",
